WIN(1)                         P32W Unix                          WIN(1)

NAME
       win - start a program in a window of its own

SYNOPSIS
       win [-g WxH] [-s COLSxROWS] [-n NAME] [-t] command [argument ...]

DESCRIPTION
       Under the window manager (wm(1)), win starts the command in
       a window of its own and returns at once: the program runs
       beside whatever else is running, on a terminal of its own.

       A program that draws with the graphics library (gfx(3)) has
       its picture's window, named after it, and no other: its
       terminal gets a window only when the program writes
       something. The keys go to the program while one of its
       windows is in front, and it may ask where the mouse is.
       The box of its picture's window ends it (it is hung up),
       and its windows go when it ends.

       The command and its arguments are run as they are given,
       in the directory win was run in.

OPTIONS
       -g WxH         the size of the program's picture, in dots
                      (400x300 without it)
       -s COLSxROWS   the size of its terminal (64x16)
       -n NAME        the name on its windows (the command's own
                      name without it)
       -t             its terminal has a window from the start

EXAMPLES
       win clock
       win -g 320x200 basic plot.bas
       win -t -s 80x24 vi notes

DIAGNOSTICS
       win: not in a window of wm, when it is run anywhere else.

SEE ALSO
       wm(1), gfx(3)
